00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:07.333
Today, instead,
we're going to talk

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.769
about metals and
metallic bonding,

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:11.670
and the properties
of metals that

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.441
are related to the kind of
bond that you get in metals.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:23.917
And you know, we kind
of motivated band theory

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:27.920
of solids with conductivity and
we talked about conductivity.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.990
There is a chart of
conductivity and there's

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:35.595
some elements in there, some
elements and some materials

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:38.164
that are more complicated,
right, like glass

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:40.800
isn't necessarily one
element or silica.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.402
There's diamond, silicon,
germanium, copper.

00:01:43.402 --> 00:01:46.940
And we talked about
insulators, semiconductors.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:50.210
And I've mentioned
metals and that's

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.145
what I want to focus on today.

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:54.848
We did this in the context
of the bands, right?

00:01:54.848 --> 00:02:01.087
So remember, here you've
got your conduction band,

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:02.889
and here you've got
your valence band,

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:04.891
and the valence band is filled.

00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:07.393
And so if there's
a large gap, that's

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:11.698
the gap, if it's large like,
you know, over 3 and 1/2

00:02:11.698 --> 00:02:14.167
EV then it's an insulator.

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:22.609
And then the one in between we
talked about a lot on Wednesday

00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:25.578
that, you know, if
this is your gap,

00:02:25.578 --> 00:02:32.051
and it's say
between 0 to 3.5 EV,

00:02:32.051 --> 00:02:33.586
then that's a semiconductor.

00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:35.522
And so, you know, that's
what we talked about.

00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:36.555
That's in your goody bag.

00:02:36.555 --> 00:02:38.625
We talked a lot
about semiconductors.

00:02:38.625 --> 00:02:40.894
And then we talked
about how, well,

00:02:40.894 --> 00:02:46.733
if you have some kind of band
that is filled in the middle

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:47.233
there.

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:49.002
So it doesn't have to
be filled in the middle.

00:02:49.002 --> 00:02:49.936
It can be filled here.

00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:51.371
It can be filled up here.

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:53.973
But the point is that
the filling of electrons

00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:58.378
does not stop where there is
a forbidden zone of states,

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:00.780
right, where there is no states.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:01.614
That's what this is.

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:02.549
That's what a gap is.

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:06.686
It's a gap of electron
freedom, right.

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:08.955
Where there's metal,
there is no such thing

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:10.890
because you fill
up to a point where

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.993
then right above that
infinitesimally tiny bit

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:16.863
of energy away, there
is a free state right?

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:19.532
There's a free state, so
you're in the middle of a band

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:22.502
and that's a metal.

00:03:22.502 --> 00:03:24.837
Well, I mean, this is you know--

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:26.406
so we should say
that the gap here

00:03:26.406 --> 00:03:27.974
has to be greater
than zero, here

00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:29.943
the gap would be equal to zero.

00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:30.677
There is no gap.

00:03:30.677 --> 00:03:34.414
You can't define
a gap in a metal.

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:36.249
And we talked about
semi-conductor problems.

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:40.286
I looked up, just for fun,
you know, I mean, why not?

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:42.622
Because what else do
you do late at night?

00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:45.692
You look up old exam problems
and you play with them.

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:48.695
And you know, OK, so from
last exam, all right,

00:03:48.695 --> 00:03:51.764
so this is for the
semiconductors, we had--

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:53.633
oh, here's a good question.

00:03:53.633 --> 00:04:02.041
2.8 centimeters cubed
of pure germanium.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:03.876
Now I want to highlight
this because there's

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:07.146
one more thing I want to say
about semiconductors before I

00:04:07.146 --> 00:04:08.881
move on.

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:10.583
Now, it's doped.

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:12.252
And that's what we
spent a lot of time

00:04:12.252 --> 00:04:13.386
talking about on Wednesday.

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:22.895
It's doped with 50
micrograms, OK, of selenium.

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:25.565
That was the problem.

00:04:25.565 --> 00:04:26.933
And the first part
of the problem

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:31.871
is, how many carriers
are produced?

00:04:38.745 --> 00:04:42.048
And in the same question,
it's like, are they n-type

00:04:42.048 --> 00:04:43.883
or are they p-type?

00:04:43.883 --> 00:04:44.884
What type of carriers?

00:04:44.884 --> 00:04:47.287
Those are things we can
answer based on what

00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:49.589
we learned on Wednesday.

00:04:49.589 --> 00:04:52.692
And so, you know, oh,
then you have this.

00:04:52.692 --> 00:04:55.528
And you say, aha, I know where
to start because everything

00:04:55.528 --> 00:04:57.930
starts here, right?

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.767
And so pure germanium is doped.

00:05:00.767 --> 00:05:02.735
Well, first of all, where am I?

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:04.537
This is my map, right?

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:06.406
Germanium is there.

00:05:06.406 --> 00:05:08.675
Now, it's doped with selenium.

00:05:08.675 --> 00:05:11.778
Is selenium to the right or
to the left of the period?

00:05:11.778 --> 00:05:13.579
It's to the right.

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:14.180
Right?

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:18.618
And so right away you know,
if you put a selenium in there

00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:21.788
instead of germanium, so if
you take a germanium atom

00:05:21.788 --> 00:05:24.090
and you put a
selenium atom in, it's

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.625
going to-- if it's
a doping, it's

00:05:25.625 --> 00:05:27.860
going to be in the
bonding network

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:30.997
that germanium was, but
add electrons to it,

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:32.031
if it's to the right.

00:05:32.031 --> 00:05:32.765
Right?

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:37.103
And so you know that it's
going to be n-type material.

00:05:37.103 --> 00:05:45.244
And if you do the math,
the number of carriers

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:51.017
is equal to 7.6 times 10 to
the 20th per centimeter cubed.

00:05:51.017 --> 00:05:55.488
Now, here's something important.

00:05:55.488 --> 00:05:58.791
That is twice as
many selenium atoms,

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:03.629
twice as many because it's
two over from germanium.

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:08.034
So it had two extra electrons
for each dopant atom, right?

00:06:08.034 --> 00:06:09.535
So the way you solve
these problems,

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:12.171
you figure out how many selenium
atoms are there per centimeter

00:06:12.171 --> 00:06:14.741
cubed and how many
electrons for each of those

00:06:14.741 --> 00:06:17.210
acted as a free carrier dope.

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:21.047
As a dopant, remember,
dopant simply

00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:25.451
give you carriers either
of their-- if it's n-type,

00:06:25.451 --> 00:06:28.054
or down here, if it's p-type.

00:06:28.054 --> 00:06:29.522
OK?

00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:31.290
And this is n-type.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.126
N-type.

00:06:33.126 --> 00:06:33.626
OK.

00:06:33.626 --> 00:06:37.063
So that's nice-- now the
reason I want to do this is

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:38.698
because, again, it is--

00:06:38.698 --> 00:06:42.502
you can never have enough
fun doing problems like this

00:06:42.502 --> 00:06:44.704
and looking up old
exams and stuff.

00:06:44.704 --> 00:06:46.472
But I also want to go
back to this picture

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:49.308
that I drew for you
on Wednesday, OK?

00:06:49.308 --> 00:06:57.617
So this picture was of
the number of carriers,

00:06:57.617 --> 00:07:02.755
and remember these are electrons
that carry current, right?

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:06.058
And we sort of drew something
that looked like this.

00:07:06.058 --> 00:07:11.831
Now remember, this is, right,
these are thermally activated

00:07:11.831 --> 00:07:20.206
and this is thermally activated,
and these are from dopants.

00:07:24.577 --> 00:07:30.283
Now, if I put dopants
in, and then I

00:07:30.283 --> 00:07:32.852
can get you a constant
number of carriers

00:07:32.852 --> 00:07:35.621
because it's just defined
by how much of the impurity

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:37.056
I've mixed in.

00:07:37.056 --> 00:07:39.959
How much impurity and I can
control that really carefully,

00:07:39.959 --> 00:07:41.994
which means I can control
the number of carriers,

00:07:41.994 --> 00:07:45.264
and then eventually,
thermally excited carriers

00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:47.332
will just start to dominate.

00:07:47.332 --> 00:07:51.237
But this ability to
dope with chemistry

00:07:51.237 --> 00:07:53.339
is what enabled the
semiconductor revolution.

00:07:53.339 --> 00:07:57.977
Now, you say, OK, what's
going on over here?

00:07:57.977 --> 00:08:00.046
What's going on
in this part here?

00:08:00.046 --> 00:08:00.713
All right.

00:08:00.713 --> 00:08:03.649
Well, you know what's
going on there because we

00:08:03.649 --> 00:08:06.519
drew this picture on Wednesday.

00:08:06.519 --> 00:08:10.890
Because if I have an
n-type, if that's my VBN

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:14.026
and that's my CBN,
the way this works

00:08:14.026 --> 00:08:18.097
is you introduce a donor level.

00:08:18.097 --> 00:08:20.132
Right?

00:08:20.132 --> 00:08:22.668
That's how doping works, right?

00:08:22.668 --> 00:08:26.939
The electrons from selenium
go into here and then

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:30.176
you have enough thermal
energy to get them up to there

00:08:30.176 --> 00:08:32.211
and that's where
they're free carriers.

00:08:32.211 --> 00:08:34.981
That's where they can
carry electricity.

00:08:34.981 --> 00:08:39.719
And so at a certain
temperature below which, right,

00:08:39.719 --> 00:08:42.488
at a certain temperature,
if you're lower than that,

00:08:42.488 --> 00:08:44.757
well there's not even
enough thermal energy

00:08:44.757 --> 00:08:46.759
to get from here to here.

00:08:46.759 --> 00:08:47.660
All right.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:48.995
Not even enough to do that.

00:08:48.995 --> 00:08:50.396
So it's got to go
down eventually.

00:08:50.396 --> 00:08:52.298
That's what that part is.

00:08:52.298 --> 00:08:55.801
And then the one other thing
I want to say about this plot

00:08:55.801 --> 00:08:57.703
is that we have names for this.

00:08:57.703 --> 00:09:03.209
These are called intrinsic
carriers, intrinsic,

00:09:03.209 --> 00:09:07.113
and these are called extrinsic.

00:09:07.113 --> 00:09:07.613
Why?

00:09:07.613 --> 00:09:09.048
I mean, that kind
of makes sense.

00:09:09.048 --> 00:09:11.617
That's actually a pretty
good way to name them.

00:09:11.617 --> 00:09:13.786
Because, you know,
intrinsic means

00:09:13.786 --> 00:09:15.021
I didn't need to do anything.

00:09:15.021 --> 00:09:19.825
It's pure germanium, pure
germanium, intrinsic.

00:09:19.825 --> 00:09:23.029
I didn't do anything
to the material.

00:09:23.029 --> 00:09:24.830
But it's still--
you can get carriers

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:27.233
from thermal activation
above the gap.

00:09:27.233 --> 00:09:30.169
Whereas if it's extrinsic, it
means I did something external.

00:09:30.169 --> 00:09:31.237
I added dopants.

00:09:31.237 --> 00:09:34.140
Those are extrinsically doped.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:37.843
So those are just important
distinguishing factors.

00:09:37.843 --> 00:09:40.413
Now, I'm going to come
back to this plot later.

00:09:40.413 --> 00:09:41.581
I want to talk about metals.

00:09:41.581 --> 00:09:43.182
Metals are all
the way over here.

00:09:43.182 --> 00:09:45.184
Those are semiconductors
and doping them

00:09:45.184 --> 00:09:47.753
or thermal activation
gets me carriers,

00:09:47.753 --> 00:09:49.989
which gets me this
connectivity range,

00:09:49.989 --> 00:09:53.359
but metals are much,
much, much higher.

00:09:53.359 --> 00:09:54.794
Metals are much,
much, much higher.

00:09:54.794 --> 00:09:55.628
Why?

00:09:55.628 --> 00:09:59.465
What is going on in
metals that's so special?

00:09:59.465 --> 00:10:00.399
OK.

00:10:00.399 --> 00:10:05.171
Well, the way we understand
everything is from the bonds.

00:10:05.171 --> 00:10:07.006
So what is happening in a metal?

00:10:07.006 --> 00:10:08.608
There's aluminum.

00:10:08.608 --> 00:10:09.875
There's aluminum.

00:10:09.875 --> 00:10:11.344
There's aluminum.

00:10:11.344 --> 00:10:12.345
And there is aluminum.

00:10:12.345 --> 00:10:14.814
For us, this is all we see now.

00:10:14.814 --> 00:10:16.549
We don't see any
of this anymore.

00:10:16.549 --> 00:10:20.019
We only see atoms and electrons
because those are the building

00:10:20.019 --> 00:10:22.788
blocks, right?

00:10:22.788 --> 00:10:24.423
Those are the building blocks.

00:10:24.423 --> 00:10:28.594
And the thing is
that, if we write down

00:10:28.594 --> 00:10:31.030
all the orbitals of an aluminum
atom, there's the core,

00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:32.665
there's a shell,
there's another shell,

00:10:32.665 --> 00:10:36.802
there's these ones out
here, way out here, right,

00:10:36.802 --> 00:10:39.271
in the case of aluminum,
three of them that are really,

00:10:39.271 --> 00:10:41.107
really, kind of weakly bonded.

00:10:41.107 --> 00:10:41.607
Right?

00:10:41.607 --> 00:10:42.540
They're way out there.

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:44.343
They're loose.

00:10:44.343 --> 00:10:48.080
They have the ability
to come off easily.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:50.449
We talked about this in the
context of other properties,

00:10:50.449 --> 00:10:52.018
right?

00:10:52.018 --> 00:10:57.456
You know, the polarizability,
the electronegativity, right?

00:10:57.456 --> 00:10:58.758
All of this feeds in.

00:10:58.758 --> 00:11:04.030
Everything is determined by
where these electrons are.

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:07.633
And then also, as I mentioned,
by how the atom's arranged.

00:11:07.633 --> 00:11:10.536
That comes next week.

00:11:10.536 --> 00:11:13.439
But if they're really,
really kind of almost free,

00:11:13.439 --> 00:11:15.207
then you can kind of
picture it like this.

00:11:15.207 --> 00:11:17.543
Let's dial it back to sodium.

00:11:17.543 --> 00:11:22.782
And you can kind of picture a
metal as truly free electrons,

00:11:22.782 --> 00:11:23.282
right.

00:11:23.282 --> 00:11:25.284
They are truly free.

00:11:25.284 --> 00:11:27.586
They're not in-- they're
in the middle of that band

00:11:27.586 --> 00:11:30.089
and they've got all this freedom
because the states are free

00:11:30.089 --> 00:11:33.292
all around them, and
they're basically not really

00:11:33.292 --> 00:11:36.696
that attached to any
one atom or another.

00:11:36.696 --> 00:11:40.533
And this is really the
picture of the metallic bond.

00:11:40.533 --> 00:11:42.935
So let's use--
let's go over here.

00:11:48.007 --> 00:11:51.811
They're so free when these atoms
come together with those very,

00:11:51.811 --> 00:11:54.246
very loosely bound
outer electrons,

00:11:54.246 --> 00:11:57.616
those electrons are so free
we actually call it a sea.

00:11:57.616 --> 00:11:59.518
We call it the electron sea.

00:11:59.518 --> 00:12:01.887
It's a simplified way
to think about it,

00:12:01.887 --> 00:12:05.091
but it's a good
picture to start with.

00:12:05.091 --> 00:12:09.428
So in metals, you've
got this electron sea.

00:12:09.428 --> 00:12:12.932
Electron sea, we'll
call it a model

00:12:12.932 --> 00:12:15.101
because it's a way of thinking.

00:12:15.101 --> 00:12:25.344
And in this model, you've got
a positive charge, nuclei,

00:12:25.344 --> 00:12:26.879
so you've got
positively charged.

00:12:26.879 --> 00:12:27.379
Why?

00:12:27.379 --> 00:12:30.249
Because they lost an
electron to the sea.

00:12:30.249 --> 00:12:31.383
They've lost it.

00:12:31.383 --> 00:12:32.985
Those outer ones have gone.

00:12:32.985 --> 00:12:35.521
Well, they're not gone because
there are others around too,

00:12:35.521 --> 00:12:38.491
but they're all just
kind of roaming free,

00:12:38.491 --> 00:12:43.529
and so you're left with these
positive charges, right?

00:12:43.529 --> 00:12:45.564
And these are the cations.

00:12:45.564 --> 00:12:47.767
Positively charged
nuclei are cations.

00:12:50.703 --> 00:12:51.203
Right.

00:12:51.203 --> 00:12:53.873
And they're in a
sea of electrons

00:12:53.873 --> 00:13:02.047
and so this is because the
valence electrons is not

00:13:02.047 --> 00:13:02.982
tightly bound.

00:13:02.982 --> 00:13:08.721
This is what I've been saying,
but this is why you get it.

00:13:08.721 --> 00:13:10.756
Cations, so positive
charge nuclei,

00:13:10.756 --> 00:13:13.492
these are cat islands
in a sea of electrons.

00:13:18.798 --> 00:13:23.202
So if you think about
a metal in this way,

00:13:23.202 --> 00:13:27.373
you understand a lot of
the properties of metals.

00:13:27.373 --> 00:13:30.743
This is a very useful way
to think about metals.

00:13:30.743 --> 00:13:34.079
And let's go back to
our pictures of bonding.

00:13:34.079 --> 00:13:35.548
Let me use this board here.

00:13:35.548 --> 00:13:37.983
I'll put it right under here.

00:13:37.983 --> 00:13:41.220
And we're going to
finish what we started.

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:42.822
We're going to finish
