The electrical conductivity
in these three elements

00:26:09.367 --> 00:26:13.138
is super high, and this is why.

00:26:13.138 --> 00:26:16.575
Because in each case, you're
going down but you're filling

00:26:16.575 --> 00:26:17.576
the d's.

00:26:17.576 --> 00:26:21.146
Oh, and then you're
even filling f's.

00:26:21.146 --> 00:26:22.614
Screening.

00:26:22.614 --> 00:26:24.449
Further away.

00:26:24.449 --> 00:26:27.519
Really free s-electrons.

00:26:27.519 --> 00:26:29.387
High conductivity.

00:26:29.387 --> 00:26:32.490
Electron sea, right?

00:26:32.490 --> 00:26:34.259
Very easy to push them around.

00:26:34.259 --> 00:26:34.759
Now.

00:26:34.759 --> 00:26:35.260
OK.

00:26:37.729 --> 00:26:42.901
This also goes back to this
picture, because, remember,

00:26:42.901 --> 00:26:45.637
I snuck it in because
it was on the graph,

00:26:45.637 --> 00:26:47.672
and I didn't take it out.

00:26:47.672 --> 00:26:50.342
But there was a
picture of silicon,

00:26:50.342 --> 00:26:53.111
where I showed
silicon increasing

00:26:53.111 --> 00:26:56.281
its intrinsic carrier
concentration with temperature,

00:26:56.281 --> 00:26:57.882
and on top of that was a metal.

00:26:57.882 --> 00:26:59.651
I think was tungsten.

00:26:59.651 --> 00:27:03.321
Now if you draw the
metal, it would be way up.

00:27:03.321 --> 00:27:06.491
It would be way up right
here because this is higher,

00:27:06.491 --> 00:27:07.759
but it went this way.

00:27:10.595 --> 00:27:12.797
But now you know why.

00:27:12.797 --> 00:27:15.367
It's totally different.

00:27:15.367 --> 00:27:18.770
The reason is totally different
than why this changes,

00:27:18.770 --> 00:27:22.540
but you can understand it
for the same root, which is

00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:24.442
the behavior of the electrons.

00:27:24.442 --> 00:27:27.345
So, in a metal, in
a semiconductor,

00:27:27.345 --> 00:27:30.382
this temperature dependence--

00:27:30.382 --> 00:27:32.384
here, I've taken the
temperature dependence away

00:27:32.384 --> 00:27:33.818
because I've doped it.

00:27:33.818 --> 00:27:36.921
But here, that's the
thermal activation

00:27:36.921 --> 00:27:42.460
into a place of freedom,
into a place where they can

00:27:42.460 --> 00:27:44.429
be free in the conduction band.

00:27:44.429 --> 00:27:46.731
Here, they're
already totally free.

00:27:46.731 --> 00:27:47.565
So what's happening?

00:27:47.565 --> 00:27:49.434
Well, when I increase
the temperature,

00:27:49.434 --> 00:27:53.104
I'm increasing the vibrations.

00:27:53.104 --> 00:27:55.774
You can imagine if these
electrons are trying

00:27:55.774 --> 00:27:59.811
to do their thing, and
they're in a sea of electrons,

00:27:59.811 --> 00:28:02.047
the last thing I want to
do if I'm moving along

00:28:02.047 --> 00:28:06.818
is to see some big
ion moving around.

00:28:06.818 --> 00:28:11.656
And if it moves more, then it's
going to make me more upset

00:28:11.656 --> 00:28:14.726
and get in my way.

00:28:14.726 --> 00:28:17.128
That's temperature.

00:28:17.128 --> 00:28:19.998
That's why this goes
down and not up.

00:28:19.998 --> 00:28:23.101
For metals, the more
thermal energy you put in,

00:28:23.101 --> 00:28:24.235
the more those ions--

00:28:24.235 --> 00:28:27.338
those positive charges--
they're positive--

00:28:27.338 --> 00:28:30.175
are going to interfere with
the electrons being free.

00:28:33.078 --> 00:28:35.714
Oh, we are understanding things.

00:28:35.714 --> 00:28:37.882
We understood why these have--

00:28:37.882 --> 00:28:39.918
now, you might say, why
are these different?

00:28:39.918 --> 00:28:42.087
That's complicated.

00:28:42.087 --> 00:28:44.089
Relativity.

00:28:44.089 --> 00:28:45.924
Not part of this class.

00:28:45.924 --> 00:28:48.326
But if you want to understand
why these are different from

00:28:48.326 --> 00:28:50.595
a-- they're all really high--

00:28:50.595 --> 00:28:53.531
why are they different, it goes
beyond the scope of this class,

00:28:53.531 --> 00:28:55.100
but it's also a very
interesting story

00:28:55.100 --> 00:29:00.538
that has to do with, on the
one hand, relativistic effects.

00:29:00.538 --> 00:29:03.174
Now there's another thing,
which is that if I go back

00:29:03.174 --> 00:29:05.510
to that table and I did this--

00:29:05.510 --> 00:29:06.544
I really did this a lot--

00:29:06.544 --> 00:29:12.217
I clicked back and forth between
electrical-- now look up here--

00:29:12.217 --> 00:29:15.186
thermal, electrical,
thermal, electrical.

00:29:15.186 --> 00:29:18.389
And you can click-- you can
go, mm, mm, mm, mm, and you can

00:29:18.389 --> 00:29:21.826
watch the table, and do that
for like five minutes or so.

00:29:21.826 --> 00:29:26.531
And what you'll find is
almost nothing changes.

00:29:26.531 --> 00:29:28.900
You can't do that with
very many properties.

00:29:28.900 --> 00:29:31.536
But for metals, and
those two properties,

00:29:31.536 --> 00:29:33.404
they're almost identical.

00:29:33.404 --> 00:29:36.541
Again, it goes back to the sea.

00:29:36.541 --> 00:29:42.914
It goes back to the sea, because
if you have electrons that

00:29:42.914 --> 00:29:49.521
are free in a material, those
can carry not just electricity,

00:29:49.521 --> 00:29:51.856
but those can be the
dominant carriers

00:29:51.856 --> 00:29:53.158
of thermal energy as well.

00:29:53.158 --> 00:29:54.959
And I'm not going to
go into those details.

00:29:54.959 --> 00:29:56.294
I just want to mention it.

00:29:56.294 --> 00:30:00.698
So this is actually called
the Wiedemann-Franz law,

00:30:00.698 --> 00:30:11.910
and it basically says for
metals, that the thermal--

00:30:11.910 --> 00:30:12.844
let's see.

00:30:12.844 --> 00:30:14.479
OK, I'll just write it here.

00:30:14.479 --> 00:30:25.156
Thermal divided by electrical
conductivity for both--

00:30:25.156 --> 00:30:29.494
conductivities-- OK, I'm
putting it in parentheses.

00:30:29.494 --> 00:30:31.696
It should be thermal
conductivity divided

00:30:31.696 --> 00:30:37.735
by electoral conductivity
goes as some constant times

00:30:37.735 --> 00:30:41.239
the temperature.

00:30:41.239 --> 00:30:44.409
So if you look at that--

00:30:44.409 --> 00:30:46.411
so you can look at it by
toggling back and forth

00:30:46.411 --> 00:30:48.213
and see how similar these are.

00:30:48.213 --> 00:30:50.949
You can also just plot stuff
or find plots of stuff.

00:30:50.949 --> 00:30:51.749
Here's one.

00:30:51.749 --> 00:30:54.719
Electroconductivity-- this is
at some fixed temperature--

00:30:54.719 --> 00:30:57.589
orders of magnitude variation,
orders of magnitude variation,

00:30:57.589 --> 00:30:59.457
tons and tons of
different materials.

00:30:59.457 --> 00:31:01.793
This is the Wiedemann-Franz law.

00:31:01.793 --> 00:31:05.697
Now you know that law is
simply a result of the fact

00:31:05.697 --> 00:31:09.868
that these free electrons
can carry both electrical--

00:31:09.868 --> 00:31:13.805
their charge-- as well
as thermal energy,

00:31:13.805 --> 00:31:15.173
and that the way
they carry those

00:31:15.173 --> 00:31:19.077
are dependent on the material,
dependent on the number

00:31:19.077 --> 00:31:21.145
of electrons, and the way
they bond and all that,

00:31:21.145 --> 00:31:23.081
but if they carry it
in one way for one,

00:31:23.081 --> 00:31:26.551
it carries it the same
way for the other.

00:31:26.551 --> 00:31:27.785
And look at all these things.

00:31:27.785 --> 00:31:32.190
Now notice here, this word
that's used a lot-- alloy.

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:33.491
Alloy.

00:31:33.491 --> 00:31:33.992
Alloy.

00:31:33.992 --> 00:31:36.995
These are all-- alloy.

00:31:36.995 --> 00:31:38.997
And I mentioned the
word alloy on Wednesday,

00:31:38.997 --> 00:31:45.270
because I said if you wanted
to make a good blue LED,

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:47.238
you had gallium nitride
but it was a little too

00:31:47.238 --> 00:31:51.476
high in its band gap, so
they alloyed it with other--

00:31:51.476 --> 00:31:53.778
then that meant mixing in.

00:31:53.778 --> 00:31:57.482
Well, that is done for metals.

00:31:57.482 --> 00:32:02.120
That is done for metals
in a very wide range.

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:02.887
Why?

00:32:02.887 --> 00:32:05.490
Because the metal
properties that you have,

00:32:05.490 --> 00:32:10.428
you may like some of, but
you may not like others.

00:32:10.428 --> 00:32:15.667
So like for copper, it's got
this really high conductivity,

00:32:15.667 --> 00:32:18.703
but maybe you don't want
it to be so malleable.

00:32:18.703 --> 00:32:20.004
What does that mean?

00:32:20.004 --> 00:32:22.073
We'll see in 10 minutes.

00:32:22.073 --> 00:32:24.842
Malleable, ductile.

00:32:24.842 --> 00:32:25.610
So what do you do?

00:32:25.610 --> 00:32:27.111
Well, you alloy it.

00:32:27.111 --> 00:32:28.613
And so here's an example.

00:32:28.613 --> 00:32:30.248
Brass.

00:32:30.248 --> 00:32:33.384
Well, we know brass,
but now you know brass.

00:32:33.384 --> 00:32:34.652
Now you really know brass.

00:32:34.652 --> 00:32:35.586
You didn't know brass.

00:32:35.586 --> 00:32:38.323
You don't know
anyone or anything

00:32:38.323 --> 00:32:40.959
until you see it as
an atom and electrons.

00:32:40.959 --> 00:32:43.328
That is how you know someone.

00:32:43.328 --> 00:32:44.128
Copper?

00:32:44.128 --> 00:32:46.431
With zinc substituted
in a two-to-one ratio--

00:32:46.431 --> 00:32:47.398
copper to zinc--

00:32:47.398 --> 00:32:49.067
the connectivity drops.

00:32:49.067 --> 00:32:53.071
Those electrons are not as free,
but they're still pretty free.

00:32:53.071 --> 00:32:55.173
Maybe they're a little more
tied up in the bonding,

00:32:55.173 --> 00:32:57.642
and maybe what you've
done by alloying

00:32:57.642 --> 00:32:59.944
has changed its
mechanical properties,

00:32:59.944 --> 00:33:02.246
because I kind of want
something copper-like

00:33:02.246 --> 00:33:05.450
but I wanted to have different
mechanical properties,

00:33:05.450 --> 00:33:07.151
so I alloyed it.

00:33:07.151 --> 00:33:11.823
And this is really how we
think about materials design.

00:33:11.823 --> 00:33:14.225
I need something
to have, let's say,

00:33:14.225 --> 00:33:15.393
a certain set of properties.

00:33:15.393 --> 00:33:17.395
Well, I don't have
them all here,

00:33:17.395 --> 00:33:19.330
and I don't have them
all here, but how can I

00:33:19.330 --> 00:33:22.300
use a combination?

00:33:22.300 --> 00:33:24.602
How can I use a combination
to get what I want?

00:33:24.602 --> 00:33:26.604
Bronze is copper with tin--

00:33:26.604 --> 00:33:28.139
four-to-one ratio
of copper and tin.

00:33:28.139 --> 00:33:31.142
The conductivity drop even more.

00:33:31.142 --> 00:33:33.044
It's not so simple.

00:33:33.044 --> 00:33:35.580
It's not so simple as,
oh, if it's four-to-one,

00:33:35.580 --> 00:33:36.981
then maybe it's like 20%.

00:33:36.981 --> 00:33:40.885
No, because it has
to do with what

00:33:40.885 --> 00:33:44.255
happens to the freedom
of these electrons.

00:33:44.255 --> 00:33:47.291
That's what it has to do with,
and that's more complicated.

00:33:47.291 --> 00:33:49.460
But maybe I've changed the
properties in another way

00:33:49.460 --> 00:33:51.396
that I really like.

00:33:51.396 --> 00:33:52.697
Well, those are substitutional.

00:33:52.697 --> 00:33:55.833
Substitutional alloys means
I took one of the coppers out

00:33:55.833 --> 00:33:57.835
and I replaced it
with something else.

00:33:57.835 --> 00:33:59.670
Here's brass with zinc in there.

00:33:59.670 --> 00:34:02.807
You can also have
interstitial alloys,

00:34:02.807 --> 00:34:04.409
and interstitial alloys--

00:34:04.409 --> 00:34:07.645
so if we-- we'll be coming
back to this when we talk about

00:34:07.645 --> 00:34:08.846
crystals next week--

00:34:08.846 --> 00:34:12.617
but alloys-- actually when
we talk about defects.

00:34:12.617 --> 00:34:17.355
So you can have substitutional--
substitutional--

00:34:17.355 --> 00:34:23.094
and you can have interstitial.

00:34:23.094 --> 00:34:25.429
This is just a preview.

00:34:25.429 --> 00:34:27.465
Interstitial.

00:34:27.465 --> 00:34:28.399
This is just a preview.

00:34:28.399 --> 00:34:32.937
We're not going to
be testing on this

00:34:32.937 --> 00:34:34.472
or thinking about
this much until we

00:34:34.472 --> 00:34:37.975
get to actually putting
these things in to a crystal

00:34:37.975 --> 00:34:41.012
later as defects.

00:34:41.012 --> 00:34:44.415
But right now, I just want
to make sure you see them

00:34:44.415 --> 00:34:45.183
for the first time.

00:34:45.183 --> 00:34:47.051
Substitution, interstitial.

00:34:47.051 --> 00:34:47.717
Why would you want
one or the other?

00:34:47.717 --> 00:34:49.253
Well, it depends
on what properties

00:34:49.253 --> 00:34:51.054
you're trying to change.

00:34:51.054 --> 00:34:54.058
In this case, I
really like iron.

00:34:54.058 --> 00:34:55.626
It's really cheap.

00:34:55.626 --> 00:34:59.397
It's a cool material, but
I need it to be stronger.

00:34:59.397 --> 00:35:00.631
And that's called steel.

00:35:00.631 --> 00:35:03.568
And if you just put a little bit
of carbon-- really, not much,

00:35:03.568 --> 00:35:06.337
like a percent by weight--

00:35:06.337 --> 00:35:09.607
it can dramatically change the
mechanical properties of iron.

00:35:09.607 --> 00:35:11.709
You can make it a much
stronger material.

00:35:11.709 --> 00:35:14.779
There is a massive, massive
range of properties.

00:35:14.779 --> 00:35:19.450
And part of why
metals are so tunable

00:35:19.450 --> 00:35:23.588
is because of the electron sea.

00:35:23.588 --> 00:35:25.490
I just came across
this article actually,

00:35:25.490 --> 00:35:26.591
and I thought I'd show you.

00:35:26.591 --> 00:35:28.793
This was just
literally yesterday.

00:35:28.793 --> 00:35:30.294
Was that yesterday?

00:35:30.294 --> 00:35:32.396
I thought it was an
interesting example.

00:35:32.396 --> 00:35:34.132
Car Wars: Steel
Getting Stronger,

00:35:34.132 --> 00:35:35.566
Lighter to Curb Aluminum Rise.

00:35:35.566 --> 00:35:39.237
Well, see the thing is that as
you want cars to be lighter--

00:35:39.237 --> 00:35:43.674
think fuel efficiency, or
range, if it's electric--

00:35:43.674 --> 00:35:45.042
you've got to use
lighter metals.

00:35:45.042 --> 00:35:47.445
And aluminum is lighter, but
aluminum is more than twice

00:35:47.445 --> 00:35:49.480
as much a steel.

00:35:49.480 --> 00:35:51.082
It's a lot lighter though.

00:35:51.082 --> 00:35:54.619
So there's a lot of
interest in making steel--

00:35:54.619 --> 00:35:56.954
that's just iron with
carbon interstitial--

00:35:56.954 --> 00:35:58.055
and making steel stronger.

00:35:58.055 --> 00:36:00.458
And you can see, "Newest alloy."

00:36:00.458 --> 00:36:01.926
That's what caught my attention.

00:36:01.926 --> 00:36:03.928
Newest alloy.

00:36:03.928 --> 00:36:05.663
They've come up with
something to add

00:36:05.663 --> 00:36:10.401
to iron to make its
mechanical properties really,

00:36:10.401 --> 00:36:14.405
really good at a
very, very thin layer

00:36:14.405 --> 00:36:18.509
because they need steel to
be even stronger in a much

00:36:18.509 --> 00:36:19.210
thinner layer.

00:36:19.210 --> 00:36:20.678
Otherwise, it's too heavy.

00:36:20.678 --> 00:36:23.014
And you've got to make it
really thin and really strong.

00:36:23.014 --> 00:36:25.049
Those are going to be alloys.

00:36:25.049 --> 00:36:25.583
OK.

00:36:25.583 --> 00:36:26.651
Back to our property list.

00:36:26.651 --> 00:36:29.153
Now, malleability and ductility.

00:36:29.153 --> 00:36:32.890
Why are metals
malleable and ductile?

00:36:32.890 --> 00:36:36.060
Well, I thought this was
a good place for a video

00:36:36.060 --> 00:36:37.128
because I really like it.

00:36:37.128 --> 00:36:40.464
I think this is a really simple
explanation of malleability

00:36:40.464 --> 00:36:43.801
and ductility as it relates
to the electron sea.

00:36:43.801 --> 00:36:44.769
So let's watch this.

00:36:44.769 --> 00:36:46.137
This looks like a minute long.

00:36:46.137 --> 00:36:46.804
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:36:46.804 --> 00:36:49.540
- [INAUDIBLE] shatters.

00:36:49.540 --> 00:36:52.944
And this is because the
applied force pushes light ions

00:36:52.944 --> 00:36:54.412
close together.

00:36:54.412 --> 00:36:58.182
They violently repel each other,
breaking the crystal apart.

00:36:58.182 --> 00:36:59.183
That's an ionic crystal.

00:36:59.183 --> 00:37:02.119
- In contrast, if you hit
a metal with a hammer,

00:37:02.119 --> 00:37:03.187
it doesn't break.

00:37:03.187 --> 00:37:04.922
It just dents.

00:37:04.922 --> 00:37:07.091
Metals are able to
deform form in response

00:37:07.091 --> 00:37:08.826
to an applied force.

00:37:08.826 --> 00:37:12.230
The mobile sea of electrons
shields the cations

00:37:12.230 --> 00:37:15.399
from each other, preventing
violent repulsion

00:37:15.399 --> 00:37:18.236
and allowing the
metal to change shape.

00:37:18.236 --> 00:37:21.706
The most malleable
metal is gold.

00:37:21.706 --> 00:37:24.942
A similar property to the
malleability of metals

00:37:24.942 --> 00:37:29.080
is their ability to be
pulled into long thin wires.

00:37:29.080 --> 00:37:31.882
We call this ductility.

00:37:31.882 --> 00:37:35.386
Ionic compounds are not ductile
for the same reason they

00:37:35.386 --> 00:37:37.488
are not malleable in general.

00:37:37.488 --> 00:37:41.125
If an ionic compound is
forced into a long cylinder,

00:37:41.125 --> 00:37:45.429
it breaks apart because of
the repulsion of like ions.

00:37:45.429 --> 00:37:48.299
In contrast, a
metal can be pulled

00:37:48.299 --> 00:37:52.036
into a long cylindrical
shape because the cations can

00:37:52.036 --> 00:37:54.472
line up, shielded
from each other,

00:37:54.472 --> 00:37:57.708
as the fluid-like like sea of
electrons flows around them.

00:37:57.708 --> 00:37:58.709
That's my favorite part.

00:37:58.709 --> 00:37:59.910
- The most ductile metal--

00:37:59.910 --> 00:38:00.478
[END PLAYBACK]

00:38:00.478 --> 00:38:02.079
Fluid-like sea.

00:38:02.079 --> 00:38:02.613
Oh.

00:38:02.613 --> 00:38:04.615
The most ductile metal.

00:38:04.615 --> 00:38:06.517
What is it?

00:38:06.517 --> 00:38:09.186
We may never know.

00:38:09.186 --> 00:38:11.622
We may-- it's platinum.

00:38:11.622 --> 00:38:12.990
But see, now, OK.

00:38:12.990 --> 00:38:13.491
OK.

00:38:13.491 --> 00:38:14.392
Now that's a--

00:38:14.392 --> 00:38:15.526
I just gave it away.

00:38:15.526 --> 00:38:17.728
OK.

00:38:17.728 --> 00:38:21.299
Ductile, malleable,
sea of electrons.

00:38:21.299 --> 00:38:23.167
Sea of electrons.

00:38:23.167 --> 00:38:25.336
Because did you see
them flowing around?

00:38:25.336 --> 00:38:29.440
You're moving the atom--
that was like a dance thing.

00:38:29.440 --> 00:38:31.142
That can be like
a new dance move

00:38:31.142 --> 00:38:33.644
that you try out tonight, the
sea of electrons dance move.

00:38:33.644 --> 00:38:36.047
I'm just saying.

00:38:36.047 --> 00:38:37.248
I'm not going to do it again.

00:38:37.248 --> 00:38:42.119
But the sea of electrons is
able to move around the ions

00:38:42.119 --> 00:38:44.455
as they're distorting
because the metal bond is not

00:38:44.455 --> 00:38:46.157
so directional.

00:38:46.157 --> 00:38:48.359
The metal bond is
not so directional.

00:38:48.359 --> 00:38:49.327
Because think about it.

00:38:49.327 --> 00:38:52.129
What is holding
the metal together?

00:38:52.129 --> 00:38:55.232
It's these interactions
between the house and the kids

00:38:55.232 --> 00:38:57.902
that are roaming
all over the place.

00:38:57.902 --> 00:38:59.403
Is the kid over
there or over there?

00:38:59.403 --> 00:39:00.671
It doesn't matter.

00:39:00.671 --> 00:39:05.076
Just give me a little
bit of negative charge

00:39:05.076 --> 00:39:08.512
so I can feel an attraction.

00:39:08.512 --> 00:39:12.483
That's the metal--
that's why it is so able

00:39:12.483 --> 00:39:15.586
to deform in the way it does.

00:39:15.586 --> 00:39:17.254
Because those atoms
are moving around,

00:39:17.254 --> 00:39:19.724
and the electrons are just kind
of there as this happy sea,

00:39:19.724 --> 00:39:21.459
and the bonding remains.

00:39:21.459 --> 00:39:23.294
It doesn't break.

00:39:23.294 --> 00:39:24.095
That's the key.

00:39:24.095 --> 00:39:26.530
Otherwise, this thing
would just crack.

00:39:26.530 --> 00:39:29.033
So wire-pulling--
check this one out.

00:39:29.033 --> 00:39:37.975
You can take a 10-centimeter
by one-centimeter platinum rod,

00:39:37.975 --> 00:39:40.845
and you can spin that into
a wire, and this is done--

00:39:40.845 --> 00:39:43.714
this is a very thin
case for effect.

00:39:43.714 --> 00:39:49.019
But you can make a
0.0006-millimeter diameter

00:39:49.019 --> 00:39:57.428
wire, and you get 2,777
kilometers of wire.

00:39:57.428 --> 00:40:00.097
Now that's because of ductility.

00:40:00.097 --> 00:40:01.732
The only reason I
can get from point A

00:40:01.732 --> 00:40:04.435
to point B there is because
I'm not breaking this apart,

00:40:04.435 --> 00:40:06.103
and that's how thin I can get--

00:40:06.103 --> 00:40:12.543
27-millimeter diameter
when I pull it.

00:40:12.543 --> 00:40:15.679
And I thought that was a
really good example of why

00:40:15.679 --> 00:40:20.651
this matters, because
maybe you've noticed,

00:40:20.651 --> 00:40:23.788
but wires are really
important to us.

00:40:23.788 --> 00:40:25.689
We use them a lot.

00:40:25.689 --> 00:40:30.094
And so, I you just saw in
a cartoon with electrons.

00:40:30.094 --> 00:40:33.964
Here is a cartoon that I
thought was interesting that

00:40:33.964 --> 00:40:36.534
shows a wire-pulling setup.

00:40:36.534 --> 00:40:39.637
So, now this is--

00:40:39.637 --> 00:40:41.372
Die, reducing the diameter.

00:40:41.372 --> 00:40:42.239
That's a die.

00:40:42.239 --> 00:40:46.043
Further reduction can take
place using extra dies.

00:40:46.043 --> 00:40:49.814
The drawn wire is
finally wound on a spool.

00:40:49.814 --> 00:40:53.117
This transformation
elongates the wire.

00:40:53.117 --> 00:40:58.122
One meter of 5.5-millimeter wire
rod can be drawn to 30 meters

00:40:58.122 --> 00:41:03.627
with one millimeter diameter
or 484 meters with 0.25

00:41:03.627 --> 00:41:05.296
millimeters diameter.

00:41:05.296 --> 00:41:05.796
OK.

00:41:05.796 --> 00:41:11.869
Now, that is a cartoon, and
I think it's always good.

00:41:11.869 --> 00:41:14.805
When you see cartoons
like this, you don't know.

00:41:14.805 --> 00:41:17.475
Go look up what
the real deal is.

00:41:17.475 --> 00:41:19.510
What does it really
look like to draw wires?

00:41:19.510 --> 00:41:21.512
And I found it pretty cool.

00:41:21.512 --> 00:41:24.849
So this is an actual
wire-drawing plant,

00:41:24.849 --> 00:41:26.350
and this is what it looks like.

00:41:26.350 --> 00:41:28.152
Now, there's no audio
here, but I just

00:41:28.152 --> 00:41:29.553
wanted to show
you the magnitude.

00:41:29.553 --> 00:41:32.890
Those are huge towers
that are melting metals

00:41:32.890 --> 00:41:37.161
to give you this first rod,
and then it goes through one

00:41:37.161 --> 00:41:37.895
after the other.

00:41:37.895 --> 00:41:39.430
And look at it being pulled--

00:41:39.430 --> 00:41:41.131
being pulled in
at some diameter,

00:41:41.131 --> 00:41:43.234
and it comes out in
a different diameter.

00:41:43.234 --> 00:41:47.738
But I wanted to show you
the scope and size of this.

00:41:47.738 --> 00:41:48.772
This is just one of them.

00:41:48.772 --> 00:41:53.344
There's a person, and so
you get a sense of the size,

00:41:53.344 --> 00:41:54.879
and then you can
also see something--

00:41:54.879 --> 00:41:56.814
when you go visit
these factories,

00:41:56.814 --> 00:41:58.549
and I have these
factories, there's

00:41:58.549 --> 00:41:59.917
always a lot of this happening.

00:41:59.917 --> 00:42:00.417
Why?

00:42:00.417 --> 00:42:03.087
Things are getting really hot.

00:42:03.087 --> 00:42:06.390
When you're spinning things like
a wire at a very high velocity,

00:42:06.390 --> 00:42:09.226
things heat up, so you get
water all over the place.

00:42:09.226 --> 00:42:13.097
So there's water being
sprayed in factories

00:42:13.097 --> 00:42:15.533
all over the place, because
you've got to keep stuff cool,

00:42:15.533 --> 00:42:19.169
or there's water baths that
you run these things through.

00:42:19.169 --> 00:42:20.871
And then you've got
to think about, well,

00:42:20.871 --> 00:42:23.274
does this thing
interact with water?

00:42:23.274 --> 00:42:24.775
How does the water play a role?

00:42:24.775 --> 00:42:27.278
It all comes down
to the electrons.

00:42:27.278 --> 00:42:29.547
And once they've actually
drawn the wire out

00:42:29.547 --> 00:42:32.116
over this huge factory,
they can actually

00:42:32.116 --> 00:42:33.083
weave it back together.

00:42:33.083 --> 00:42:33.918
So I found this.

00:42:33.918 --> 00:42:35.486
This is the same factory.

00:42:35.486 --> 00:42:38.589
Now these things here
are doing the opposite.

00:42:38.589 --> 00:42:41.725
So these are huge drums,
about the size of a person,

00:42:41.725 --> 00:42:46.697
and each of these got
a wire that was drawn.

00:42:46.697 --> 00:42:50.200
And now, again, because
of the ductility,

00:42:50.200 --> 00:42:53.203
you can also move the
wires, play with the wires,

00:42:53.203 --> 00:42:54.538
and you can weave them together.

00:42:54.538 --> 00:42:56.273
You can weave them
right back together

00:42:56.273 --> 00:42:58.542
into a thread that looks
like that, which we all have

00:42:58.542 --> 00:43:01.979
seen I think many, many times.

00:43:01.979 --> 00:43:05.482
All of this processing, all
of this manufacturability,

00:43:05.482 --> 00:43:08.052
has to do with the electron sea.

00:43:08.052 --> 00:43:10.988
That's why we have wires

00:43:10.988 --> 00:43:11.689
OK.

00:43:11.689 --> 00:43:15.159
Now, a couple more small points.

00:43:15.159 --> 00:43:18.062
So we're going to go
back to this picture

00:43:18.062 --> 00:43:19.930
because this is how we started.

00:43:22.933 --> 00:43:26.637
And this is the 1-D--

00:43:26.637 --> 00:43:33.243
remember that 1-D model that we
used to introduce band theory--

00:43:33.243 --> 00:43:39.717
and I want to go back to this,
because what you can see is

00:43:39.717 --> 00:43:47.424
that if I think about this
as bonding and anti-bonding

00:43:47.424 --> 00:43:49.927
orbitals all kind
of mixed in here,

00:43:49.927 --> 00:43:55.833
then you can also get a sense,
just like in the dimers that we

00:43:55.833 --> 00:43:56.433
make--

00:43:56.433 --> 00:44:01.238
N2, O2, remember NO?

00:44:01.238 --> 00:44:02.906
Bond order.

00:44:02.906 --> 00:44:06.910
It counts how many electrons
are in an anti-bonding orbital.

00:44:06.910 --> 00:44:08.746
Just because it's in
an anti-bonding orbital

00:44:08.746 --> 00:44:10.681
doesn't mean it's not
there in the system.

00:44:10.681 --> 00:44:13.517
It's the same with
these metal bands.

00:44:13.517 --> 00:44:15.386
Some of them may be
good for bonding.

00:44:15.386 --> 00:44:17.287
Some of them may not.

00:44:17.287 --> 00:44:20.190
But just like in the
dimers, the bond order

00:44:20.190 --> 00:44:23.594
is related to the
strength of that bond.

00:44:23.594 --> 00:44:24.395
Remember?

00:44:24.395 --> 00:44:26.497
And in NO, it's like a
bond order of 2 and 1/2,

00:44:26.497 --> 00:44:30.768
and then if I took an electron
out, it actually got stronger.

00:44:30.768 --> 00:44:32.736
The bonding got stronger.

00:44:32.736 --> 00:44:34.505
It's the same thing.

00:44:34.505 --> 00:44:37.074
You can think about this
the same way in metals.

00:44:37.074 --> 00:44:38.442
You have a band.

00:44:38.442 --> 00:44:42.312
Some of these orbitals are not
going to be bonding orbitals,

00:44:42.312 --> 00:44:45.949
and some of them are going to
be much more about the bonding.

00:44:45.949 --> 00:44:53.457
And so that's going to relate
to things like boiling point.

00:44:53.457 --> 00:44:56.527
Metals have boiling points too.

00:44:56.527 --> 00:44:59.963
So if you look now, go
back to the periodic table,

00:44:59.963 --> 00:45:03.167
remember the highest electrical
connectivity was here.

00:45:03.167 --> 00:45:08.272
Well, that had to do with that
right there, with the position,

00:45:08.272 --> 00:45:10.541
with the filling of
the orbitals to give me

00:45:10.541 --> 00:45:13.043
the freest electron
I can imagine,

00:45:13.043 --> 00:45:16.280
after those d-orbitals
are filled, that s.

00:45:16.280 --> 00:45:18.115
But if I'm thinking
about whether I filled

00:45:18.115 --> 00:45:20.017
anti-bondings orbitals
or not, because now I'm

00:45:20.017 --> 00:45:22.486
thinking about the
strength of the bonds,

00:45:22.486 --> 00:45:24.488
you see a different trend,
and you see something

00:45:24.488 --> 00:45:26.090
that you would expect.

00:45:26.090 --> 00:45:27.991
First of all, you see
a huge variability,

00:45:27.991 --> 00:45:29.893
but you see that the
ones that are strongest--

00:45:29.893 --> 00:45:31.462
the highest boiling points--

00:45:31.462 --> 00:45:34.598
are going to be the ones
where you fill that band--

00:45:34.598 --> 00:45:36.834
those bands-- roughly halfway.

00:45:39.369 --> 00:45:43.440
And this is also why,
in a metallic bond,

00:45:43.440 --> 00:45:49.146
in a metallic bond, you've
got this enormous range.

00:45:49.146 --> 00:46:02.693
So you've got-- it's
the weakest for elements

00:46:02.693 --> 00:46:13.837
with near-empty,
near empty, valence

00:46:13.837 --> 00:46:17.975
subshells, valence
subshells, and I

00:46:17.975 --> 00:46:24.515
would say, like OK,
caesium is an example,

00:46:24.515 --> 00:46:31.255
or near-full, like, for
example, in mercury.

00:46:31.255 --> 00:46:34.458
Now mercury is actually
a liquid at room

00:46:34.458 --> 00:46:36.593
temperature because of this.

00:46:36.593 --> 00:46:37.961
There it is.

00:46:37.961 --> 00:46:39.463
There it is.

00:46:39.463 --> 00:46:39.963
Why?

00:46:39.963 --> 00:46:45.569
Because I-- remember
the beryllium dimer.

00:46:45.569 --> 00:46:48.372
I filled all these
electrons in there,

00:46:48.372 --> 00:46:53.277
and they're causing this really
weak bonding, this really weak

00:46:53.277 --> 00:46:54.978
bonding, because I
had to put all of them

00:46:54.978 --> 00:46:57.581
into the anti-bonding orbitals.

00:46:57.581 --> 00:47:00.017
I couldn't do this trick
that right over here, right

00:47:00.017 --> 00:47:01.985
one column next
to it, these guys

00:47:01.985 --> 00:47:04.087
can have tricks, where they--

00:47:04.087 --> 00:47:05.689
now this is bonding strength.

00:47:05.689 --> 00:47:08.158
This is not electroconductivity.

00:47:08.158 --> 00:47:11.762
But here, they're filling the
d, and then the s is free.

00:47:11.762 --> 00:47:12.963
Not here.

00:47:12.963 --> 00:47:15.599
Here they're all filled,
bonding and anti-bonding.

00:47:15.599 --> 00:47:17.534
No tricks.

00:47:17.534 --> 00:47:21.171
Now, if you look at the
melting temperature-- that's

00:47:21.171 --> 00:47:23.173
one of the strongest
elements would be tungsten.

00:47:27.211 --> 00:47:33.350
The strongest would be
example tungsten, where

00:47:33.350 --> 00:47:36.954
you've got a range of melting--

00:47:36.954 --> 00:47:38.856
the melting point of tungsten--

00:47:38.856 --> 00:47:40.858
and this is not the same
as the boiling point--

00:47:40.858 --> 00:47:46.396
the melting point of
tungsten is 3,680 degrees C.

00:47:46.396 --> 00:47:49.566
That's a liquid at
room temperature.

00:47:49.566 --> 00:47:55.405
And the melting point
of cesium is 28 degrees

00:47:55.405 --> 00:47:57.074
C. Look at that range.

00:47:57.074 --> 00:47:59.109
And it all has to do with
how these electrons are

00:47:59.109 --> 00:48:02.012
filling the metallic bands.

00:48:02.012 --> 00:48:05.549
And more than that, because
it also has to do with the way

00:48:05.549 --> 00:48:08.919
the atoms come together,
and that is going

00:48:08.919 --> 00:48:10.520
to be the topic of next week.

00:48:10.520 --> 00:48:12.522
Now that would have
been a perfect line

00:48:12.522 --> 00:48:16.026
to end on because I said
the words, "next week."

00:48:16.026 --> 00:48:18.595
I just want to make
one more point.

00:48:18.595 --> 00:48:20.163
I can't stop because
there is one more

00:48:20.163 --> 00:48:25.602
thing I want to show very
quickly in one minute.

00:48:25.602 --> 00:48:31.441
And that is-- going
back to this picture,

00:48:31.441 --> 00:48:34.978
if you're thinking
about filling,

00:48:34.978 --> 00:48:36.446
and I've got like my 3s--

00:48:36.446 --> 00:48:40.250
remember, I've got like my 3s--

00:48:40.250 --> 00:48:47.457
and let's put 2p here
and then 3s and 3p.

00:48:47.457 --> 00:48:51.828
Now remember, for metals, for
a lot of these metals, what

00:48:51.828 --> 00:48:55.599
ends up happening is what I
showed you before on Wednesday,

00:48:55.599 --> 00:48:58.535
which is that you get
actually an overlap of this,

00:48:58.535 --> 00:49:05.776
so you get a band that
would be the 3s/3p band.

00:49:05.776 --> 00:49:09.012
Sodium, magnesium,
aluminum-- these

00:49:09.012 --> 00:49:13.583
all come together like
this to form a single band.

00:49:13.583 --> 00:49:16.620
Now remember, I don't need
you to know how to do this.

00:49:16.620 --> 00:49:19.056
We would tell you if
it does this or not.

00:49:19.056 --> 00:49:20.090
And we talked about that.

00:49:20.090 --> 00:49:23.627
I'm not going to ask you to
know how these come together,

00:49:23.627 --> 00:49:25.829
do they form a
continuous band or not?

00:49:25.829 --> 00:49:28.665
But in this case,
they do, for that row.

00:49:28.665 --> 00:49:30.100
And so you know.

00:49:30.100 --> 00:49:34.504
I've got eight electrons,
eight-electron capacity

00:49:34.504 --> 00:49:38.308
in that band per atom.

00:49:38.308 --> 00:49:39.643
That's the capacity of the band.

00:49:39.643 --> 00:49:41.878
So now I want to talk about,
how is this band filled?

00:49:41.878 --> 00:49:46.850
Well, for sodium, I'm
going to fill it 1/8th,

00:49:46.850 --> 00:49:48.752
because it's got just
one electron that it's

00:49:48.752 --> 00:49:50.620
putting into that band.

00:49:50.620 --> 00:49:57.127
And for magnesium, I'm going
to fill it with two electrons.

00:49:57.127 --> 00:49:59.629
And aluminum would be three.

00:50:03.000 --> 00:50:05.402
And so on.

00:50:05.402 --> 00:50:07.304
So it goes back to the
same picture as before.

00:50:07.304 --> 00:50:09.439
We're filling the
bands, but now we've

00:50:09.439 --> 00:50:12.476
got 10 to the 23rd
and 24th atoms,

00:50:12.476 --> 00:50:14.311
and so we think about
filling the bands

00:50:14.311 --> 00:50:20.884
as what's the occupancy per
atom you can put in there?

00:50:20.884 --> 00:50:23.220
And so I could tell you
if I had a mole of sodium,

00:50:23.220 --> 00:50:25.422
how many electrons I
have in this band, right?

00:50:25.422 --> 00:50:27.024
It's a mole.

00:50:27.024 --> 00:50:28.325
And how did you fill it?

00:50:28.325 --> 00:50:30.627
And that ties into what
I just talked about,

00:50:30.627 --> 00:50:33.463
which is how that relates
to bonding strength.

00:50:33.463 --> 00:50:33.964
OK.

00:50:33.964 --> 00:50:35.399
Now we can stop.

00:50:35.399 --> 00:50:38.568
On Monday, we will start
talking about crystals.

00:50:38.568 --> 00:50:40.937
Have a very good weekend.

